1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,680
GeoResponse turns satellite imagery into a decision a manager can defend. Not a map demo: a decision, the evidence behind it, and a human correction. In this video we use a real example case in the Ahr valley, with real Sentinel-2 images.

2
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:29,820
There are two data sources. The demo pack is the bundled synthetic scenario for a stage or booth. My workspace is your own sites. You switch in Settings, and the demo pack stays one click away.

3
00:00:30,721 --> 00:00:43,051
The workspace starts empty. Import a CSV or GeoJSON, add one site manually, or load the bundled example. This is the difference between a demo and a product you can put your own data into.

4
00:00:43,955 --> 00:00:55,955
We load the example case: six sites in the Ahr supply corridor. Each row carries coordinates, severity, confidence, value, population, access and notes.

5
00:00:56,862 --> 00:01:08,982
Every site becomes a pin and a row. Red is high risk, orange is watch. The summary gives the decision-maker the shape of the problem: six sites, how many red, value at risk, and people.

6
00:01:09,882 --> 00:01:22,762
This is not a fixed demo. Select a site and edit its name, coordinates, severity, confidence, value, population or access. Changes are saved in this browser until you connect a backend.

7
00:01:23,664 --> 00:01:33,934
Export the list as CSV or GeoJSON. The point is not to trap your data in this app; it is to move the decision into the tools your team already uses.

8
00:01:34,834 --> 00:01:50,074
The site list is one half. The other half is Watch, the proactive inbox. For this example we used the API: twelve quiet baseline values, then observations over time. The last observation crossed the threshold and produced this alert.

9
00:02:10,975 --> 00:02:26,545
Watch shows the alert, not just a pin: severity, confidence, the SLA clock, the owner, the real Sentinel before and after, the value at risk, access, and why it fired against the baseline. The human sees the evidence before acting.

10
00:02:27,445 --> 00:03:10,645
Now ask the agent. It answers from grounded tools: the portfolio briefing, this alert, and coverage. It does not guess from a prompt: it calls get_briefing, get_alert and get_coverage, and you can see each step. The answer is slower than a chat box because the work is real: it reads the alert, the baseline, the SLA and the evidence, then proposes one safe next action. Notice the answer names the alert, the baseline, the SLA and the evidence; it does not invent a number. Every write is a proposal. Nothing is approved automatically. The system never approves a claim, dispatches a crew or signs an assessment. That is the difference between an assistant and an autonomous system.

11
00:03:31,554 --> 00:03:44,604
The agent proposes assigning a team. A human approves it here. The alert changes, the action is audited, and the proposal can be undone. The system never signs, dispatches or approves a claim on its own.

12
00:03:45,507 --> 00:03:56,567
Switch back to the demo pack whenever you need the synthetic scenarios for a stage. Your workspace is untouched. Both paths exist on purpose: one to show, one to use.

13
00:03:57,467 --> 00:04:07,207
Real imagery. A ranked decision. A human correction. A proactive watch. That is GeoResponse: your sites, your evidence, your decision.
